From aa5f6def09b28c167998da49a53ca0457bbaa546 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dist <build@localhost>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 19:11:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: queue same-process user APCs without a wineserver
 round trip
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every same-process user APC costs at least two wineserver round trips
today: one to queue (dlls/ntdll/unix/thread.c:1796-1824,
NtQueueApcThreadEx2, unconditional SERVER_START_REQ(queue_apc)) and one
to drain on the waiting side even with ntsync (dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c,
zero-timeout server_wait asserting STATUS_USER_APC after an alert wake).
NtTestAlert and the TEST_ALERT branch of NtContinueEx are each another
full zero-timeout server select even when nothing is pending
(dlls/ntdll/unix/server.c:848-879). Idle Live 12 keeps ~3,650 wineserver
context switches/sec alive on an empty set through exactly this traffic.

This patch adds a client-side fast path, gated by WINE_APC_FASTPATH
(on by default; "0"/"off" disables, case-insensitive) and by ntsync
being present (inproc_device_fd >= 0); with either off, every code path
falls back to today's exact behavior.

Queue side. Each user thread registers in a process-wide registry at
init (server_init_process / server_init_thread) and deregisters at exit
(pthread_exit_wrapper, before the fds are closed, so queuers can never
touch a recycled fd number). Registration eagerly creates the thread's
alert fd (one get_inproc_alert_fd server call per thread, trivial) and a
client-owned manual-reset ntsync poke event (NTSYNC_IOC_CREATE_EVENT on
the process's /dev/ntsync instance, no server object behind it). System
threads (no TEB) are not registered: the server rejects APCs to them
with STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED, and unregistered targets keep that path.

NtQueueApcThreadEx2 takes the fast path only for plain user APCs
(flags == QUEUE_USER_APC_FLAGS_NONE, no reserve handle; special APCs
and reserves keep their server semantics). The target handle is resolved
through a generation-validated cache: on a miss, one
NtQueryInformationThread(ThreadBasicInformation) learns the pid/tid;
cross-process handles are permanently marked server-routed. Cached
handle -> {tid, generation} entries are validated against the registry
on every use; the generation is bumped per registration, and
deregistration poisons cache entries for the exiting tid, so a recycled
ptid (alloc_ptid reuses freed slots promptly) can never validate a stale
entry. On a validation failure the mapping is refreshed through a new
query. Handle recycling is covered separately: wineserver hands a freed
handle slot straight back (server/handle.c: alloc_entry starts at
table->free, close_handle lowers it), so NtClose and NtDuplicateObject's
DUPLICATE_CLOSE_SOURCE branch call apc_fastpath_forget_handle to drop
the cache entry for the closed handle before the slot can be
reallocated, following the fd cache's remove-before-close discipline
("always remove the cached fd; if the server request fails we'll just
retrieve it again"). Without this a recycled handle would validate
against the old thread and deliver its APCs there. If the target left
the registry, is a system thread, or is blocked where a poke cannot
reach it (server_select, WaitAll and full 64-handle waits,
signal-and-wait, tracked by an unarmed_wait_depth counter), the APC
falls back to today's server queue_apc.

Signal safety. Every apc_mutex section runs under
server_enter_uninterrupted_section, the way virtual.c guards
virtual_mutex: SIGUSR1 (suspend, or a system APC queued to a thread not
already in an APC wait, server/thread.c:1473-1477) re-enters the fast
path through usr1_handler -> wait_suspend -> server_select ->
apc_fastpath_enter_unarmed_wait, and the queue and fetch sides hold the
mutex across ntsync ioctls, so a signal delivered inside an unprotected
section would self-deadlock the thread with apc_mutex held for the life
of the process.

Wake protocol. The poke event mirrors the client queue state, following
the server's own alert_sync protocol (server/thread.c:1483-1488,
1520-1521, 1536-1537): queuers append the payload and set the event only
on the empty-to-non-empty transition, the owner pops and resets the
event on the non-empty-to-empty transition, both under apc_mutex, and
waiters always poll the client queue before sleeping. A poke can never
be lost between the queue-empty poll and the in-kernel wait that follows
it, and the event is never left signaled behind an empty queue (which
would otherwise turn every later wait into an instant-return loop, the
issue-109 livelock class). Self-queued APCs skip the poke entirely: the
owner cannot be blocked while running, and its next poll finds the APC.
The server's alert event stays strictly server-owned (the client never
sets or resets it), so it keeps tracking the server-side queue
(cross-process and special APCs) and remains a reliable "server queue
non-empty" predicate for the drain side. (An earlier draft of this work
poked the server's alert fd directly; that leaves the manual-reset event
signaled forever once a peer APC lands, since the server only resets it
on server-queue transitions, degrading every later in-kernel wait to an
instant wake. The dedicated poke event avoids that outright.)

Drain side. Every alertable entry point polls the client queue first and
dispatches through the existing invoke_user_apc (server.c:385), so
KiUserApcDispatcher semantics (one APC armed per call, the rest chained
through the dispatcher's KCONTINUE_FLAG_TEST_ALERT continue) are
unchanged:

- server_select (server.c:718-789) raises unarmed_wait_depth for its
  whole duration, then, for alertable calls, delivers one queued client
  APC through *user_apc (the existing callers invoke it with the right
  context, so NtTestAlert and NtContinueEx get client APCs with zero
  changes). Alertable zero-handle zero-timeout polls (NtTestAlert,
  NtContinueEx's TEST_ALERT, alert drains) skip the server request
  entirely when NTSYNC_IOC_EVENT_READ shows the alert event unsignaled (the
  server-side queue is then provably empty), returning the same
  STATUS_TIMEOUT the select would have returned. Thread termination does
  not depend on these round trips (kill_thread signals SIGQUIT,
  server/thread.c:1635), and system APCs arrive through SIGUSR1 signal
  frames, so skipping the poll is safe.
- inproc_wait arms the poke event as an extra wait object in alertable
  WaitAny waits with a free slot (count < 64), so a same-process APC
  wakes the wait in-kernel; WaitAll/full waits raise unarmed_wait_depth
  instead, which routes queuers through the server exactly like today.
  The alert branch of linux_wait_objs no longer drains internally with
  assert(ret == STATUS_USER_APC): the wake is returned raw and the
  callers drain, so the cancel race (APC cancelled between kernel wake
  and drain) now reports STATUS_USER_APC instead of crashing on the
  assert. inproc_signal_and_wait gets the same treatment via the
  unarmed_wait_depth protocol.
- The alertable sleep fast path from the previous patch arms the poke
  event as its single wait object; a client wake delivers one APC and
  returns STATUS_USER_APC, exactly the result an alert wake produces
  today.

Thread exit discards undelivered client APCs, matching Windows dropping
APCs queued to terminated threads (the server path likewise fails the
queue with STATUS_UNSUCCESSFUL once the thread is gone). Cross-process
APCs, special APCs, system APCs, thread_cancel_apc semantics, and the
alert event reset all remain server-owned.

Hosts without /dev/ntsync are short-circuited at the gate (inproc_device_fd >= 0): with no ntsync device no thread can register a poke event, so attempting the fast path there would only add a wasted NtQueryInformationThread round trip per handle to every queue_apc call.

Verification: full series plus this patch applies with git am --3way
onto the pristine base; dlls/ntdll/unix/{sync,server,thread,virtual}.o
compile warning-free with HAVE_LINUX_NTSYNC_H; ntsync count=0/count=1
alert-wait semantics, the poke event create/set/reset ioctls, and
EVENT_READ queue-state polling were exercised against /dev/ntsync on the
build host with a standalone test. The handle-reuse and suspend-stress
regressions found in review are pinned by apcprobe case7 (a closed
thread handle recycled by CreateThread must not deliver to the old
thread) and case8 (a SuspendThread/ResumeThread hammer against a queuer
and an alertable waiter, with bounded joins), which fail on the pre-fix
revision and pass here, with the gate on and off.
